    <h2>Frequently Asked Questions — Florida Closing Costs</h2>
    <% const _faqs = [["What are typical closing costs in Florida?", "Florida buyer closing costs range from 2% to 4% excluding down payment. Key FL-specific items: doc stamps on mortgage (0.35% of loan), intangible tax (0.2% of loan), and title insurance (buyer pays in most counties). On a $400,000 purchase with 20% down, total buyer closing costs typically run $7,000–$9,000 before prepaids."], ["What is Florida documentary stamp tax?", "Doc stamps on the deed are $0.70 per $100 of purchase price (paid by seller by custom in most counties). Doc stamps on the mortgage note are $0.35 per $100 of the loan (always paid by buyer). Miami-Dade uses $1.05/$100 for the deed and the buyer customarily pays it there."], ["What is the Florida intangible tax on a mortgage?", "The intangible tax is 0.002 × loan amount (0.2%). On a $320,000 mortgage it is $640. It is always paid by the borrower. VA loans are generally exempt."], ["Who pays title insurance in Florida?", "In most Florida counties the buyer pays for the owner title policy. Exceptions include Miami-Dade, Broward, Sarasota, and Collier counties where the seller customarily pays. The lender title policy is always paid by the buyer. Florida title rates are state-regulated and the same at all companies."], ["Is flood insurance required in Florida?", "Your lender requires it if the property is in a FEMA Special Flood Hazard Area (SFHA). Even outside mandatory flood zones, purchasing coverage is strongly recommended in Florida given storm surge and heavy rainfall risk. Flood insurance is separate from homeowners insurance and must be purchased from the NFIP or a private flood carrier."], ["What is the Florida homestead exemption?", "The homestead exemption reduces a primary residence assessed value by up to $50,000. The Save Our Homes cap then limits annual assessment increases to 3% or CPI (whichever is lower). Apply with your county property appraiser the year after purchase. This can significantly reduce your property tax bill from year two onward."], ["How does Miami-Dade differ from other Florida counties at closing?", "Miami-Dade has the highest doc stamp rate ($1.05/$100 vs $0.70) and by local custom the buyer pays it instead of the seller. Miami-Dade also has some of the highest homeowners insurance costs nationally due to hurricane exposure. Title insurance in Miami-Dade is paid by the seller by custom."], ["Can the seller pay my closing costs in Florida?", "Yes. Sellers can contribute toward buyer closing costs within limits set by the loan type: 3%–9% for conventional (varies by down payment), 6% for FHA, 4% for VA. Seller concessions are negotiated in the purchase contract and reduce your out-of-pocket cash at closing."]]; _faqs.forEach(function(f) { %>
